From: klizas <lukas@klizas.lt>
Subject: [PATCH] drm/appletbdrm: pad requests that are a multiple of the bulk
 max packet size

A framebuffer request whose total size is an exact multiple of the bulk
OUT endpoint's wMaxPacketSize (512) is never terminated on the wire:
usb_bulk_msg() does not send a zero-length packet and the device
delimits messages by short packets. The device's parser then waits for
the rest of the message forever, stops responding, and every later
flush stalls for APPLETBDRM_BULK_MSG_TIMEOUT until the device is reset.

Request sizes are 16-aligned, so roughly 1 in 32 damage rect sizes hits
the boundary, which is why partial updates seemed to fail under load
while full-frame updates (a constant size that is not a multiple) never
fail.

Pad such requests by 16 bytes. The device accepts trailing padding
counted in the header size: the existing ALIGN(..., 16) sends up to 15
padding bytes today.

--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/tiny/appletbdrm.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/tiny/appletbdrm.c
@@ -336,6 +336,9 @@
 		       frames_size +
 		       sizeof(struct appletbdrm_fb_request_footer), 16);
 
+	if (!(request_size % SZ_512))
+		request_size += 16;
+
 	appletbdrm_state->request = kvzalloc(request_size, GFP_KERNEL);
 
 	if (!appletbdrm_state->request)
